  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 AM MST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nPleasant seasonal weather expected on this Thanksgiving day. \nFriday looks just as pleasant. Temperatures will increase slightly\nabove seasonal normals by Saturday before winds pick up over the \nevening hours. Those winds are predominantly drawn out of the \nnorthwest as a pacific system impacts our neighboring states to \nthe north. No rain chances for us this weekend, but that \ntemperature advection will cool our area down going into the \nbeginning of next week. Shortwaves in that trough pattern will \ngenerate sustained wind speeds up to 20-25 mph out of the west \nwith the greatest gusts impacting eastern mountain slopes. The \nimpression of a ridge follows bringing lighter winds and \ntemperatures bump back up over the beginning of next week, but \nsome shortwaves in the trailing zonal pattern may create more \nbreezy conditions Monday evening.\n\nA distinct trough sets up on Wednesday, and model agreement \nincreases confidence in another chance for precipitation arriving \nthat evening. This Pacific system is expected to cool temperatures\ndown, and PWs approach 0.75\". Snow levels drop that evening, so \nsnow chances in the area mountains cannot be ruled out. The \naftermath of this storm is uncertain as some models allude to a \nbackdoor flow out of the northeast late next week, others suggest \na northwesterly flow customary with a pacific ridge following a \ntrough. Either way, anticipate a cooler start to the beginning of \nDecember.",
